\section*{Why this document exists}

\texttt{convergence.tex} proves the convergence theorem for the class
$\mathfrak{G}$ in one piece. That is the right shape for a proof and the wrong
shape for a reader: the hypotheses, the four interacting blocks and the
composition all arrive together, and there is no point before the end at which a
reader has something they can accept or reject.

This document breaks the same result into five parts. Each part switches on one
more moving object, each has a one-sentence claim, and each is a theorem that
stands alone.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}c p{0.37\textwidth} p{0.15\textwidth} p{0.10\textwidth} p{0.13\textwidth}@{}}
\hline
part & claim & switched on & needs & components\\
\hline
1 & Given the atoms, the weights are free. & categorical head &
  \emph{nothing} & any\\
2 & On a fixed landscape, one attractor per cell is what transport needs, and
  its failure is fatal. & component means & (C), (L) & any\\
3 & The blur anneals itself, and sets the bias. & scales & heat equation &
  \textbf{Gaussian}\\
4 & Let the opponent move: it is a chase, and it damps. & coupling & (D) & any\\
5 & All of them at once. & --- & (G) & any\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The structural point is that Parts 1--4 are each independently checkable, and
Part 5 --- the small-gain composition, which is the technical heart and the hard
read --- only has to be believed by someone who already believes 1--4. A reader
who bounces off Part 5 still has four theorems, a sharpness result and a
certified counterexample.

The last column is the second structural point, and it is why the parts are
ordered as they are. \emph{The choice of component family is not used until
Part 3.} Parts 1 and 2 are stated for an arbitrary family --- point masses
included --- because their proofs never open up a component: Part 1 needs only
that the per-component expectations exist, and Part 2 needs only a fixed
differentiable landscape and a preconditioned ascent step. Part 3 is where the
Gaussian is bought and paid for: it is the heat kernel, the width acquires its own
gradient, and the two facts together give self-annealing, a closed-form bias law
and the theory's sharpest numerical predictions. Parts 4 and 5 return to
indifference, inheriting the Gaussian only through Part 3.

Deferring the choice is not bookkeeping. It is what makes the role of the
Gaussian legible: read in this order, Part 3's $O(s^2)$ bias is visibly not an
unavoidable fact about mixture policies --- Part 1 exhibits a family with no bias
at all --- but the price charged for the annealing mechanism of that part.

\medskip\noindent
Proofs here are deliberately compressed to the idea; the full ones live in
\texttt{game\_class.tex} and \texttt{convergence.tex}, referenced as
\GC{Lemma~1} and \CV{Lemma~4} respectively. The exception is
\S\ref{sec:transport}, whose two results are given in full: everything in Parts
3--5 is an estimate added to Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}, so its hypotheses had
better be exactly right, and writing the proof out is what shows which they are.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Setting, in one paragraph.} Player $0$ maximises and player $1$ minimises
a bounded measurable payoff $u : \cA\times\cB \to \R$ on compact action sets.
Each player plays a $K$-component mixture with a categorical head over the
components: a weight vector $w \in \Delta_K$, and $K$ component laws with location
parameters $m_1,\dots,m_K$. \emph{Which} family the components are drawn from is
left open until Part 3, where it is fixed to diagonal Gaussians; until then the
only thing assumed is that a component is a probability measure concentrated near
its location, and that the location can be moved by a gradient step. The
\emph{effective landscape} of player $0$ is $Q_\nu(a) = \int u(a,b)\,d\nu(b)$:
the value of playing the pure action $a$ against the opponent's current policy
$\nu$. We fix a Nash equilibrium and assume throughout that it is supported on
finitely many points --- the \emph{atoms} $p_1,\dots,p_{M_0}$ for player $0$ ---
with positive weights $w^\star$.

\part*{Part 1. Given the atoms, the weights are free}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Part 1}
\setcounter{section}{1}
\setcounter{theorem}{0}

\idea{A mixture policy has two kinds of parameter, and they are doing completely
different jobs. The component means answer \emph{where are the atoms}; the
categorical head answers \emph{how much mass on each}. The second question turns
out to be a finite matrix game --- not approximately, exactly --- and finite matrix
games are solved by the algorithm already in use, globally, from any
initialisation, with no assumptions on the payoff whatsoever. So half the problem
is free, and every hypothesis this theory will ever need belongs to the other
half. Nothing in this part depends on what the components are.}

\subsection*{1.1 The decomposition}

Suppose player $0$'s components are probability measures $P_1,\dots,P_{K_0}$ on
$\cA$ with weights $w \in \Delta_{K_0}$, and player $1$'s are $R_1,\dots,R_{K_1}$
on $\cB$ with weights $v$. Define the \emph{component matrix}: the entry
$\bar A_{kl}$ is the expected payoff when player $0$ samples from its component
$k$ and player $1$ from its component $l$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Abar}
  \bar A_{kl} \;:=\; \E_{a \sim P_k,\ b \sim R_l}\big[u(a,b)\big].
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Exact decomposition, \GC{Lemma~1}]\label{lem:decomp}
For any bounded measurable $u$ and any component families,
\[
  U(\pi_{\theta_0},\pi_{\theta_1}) \;=\; w^\top \bar A\, v ,
\]
and consequently the two heads receive exactly the following gradients:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the categorical head of player $0$ sees the vector $\bar A v$, so its
  update is \emph{verbatim} tabular magnetic mirror descent on the finite
  zero-sum matrix game $\bar A$;
\item[(ii)] the location parameter of component $k$ sees $w_k \nabla_{m_k}
  \E_{a\sim P_k} Q_{\pi_{\theta_1}}(a)$: preconditioned gradient ascent on the
  effective landscape, averaged against that component's own shape.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Write the expected payoff as a double sum over the two players' components and
apply Fubini. The mixture weights factor out of the integral because they do not
depend on the sampled action, leaving a bilinear form in $(w,v)$ whose
coefficients are exactly the per-component expectations. No structure on $u$ is
used, and none on the components --- only that the expectations exist.
\end{proof}

The content is (i). The categorical head is not \emph{approximately} playing a
matrix game, or playing one in some limit; it is playing one identically, at
every step, whatever the components happen to be and wherever they happen to sit.

\begin{example}[The two families this document uses]\label{ex:families}
Two instances of \eqref{eq:Abar} are worth having in view from the start, because
the difference between them is exactly what Part 3 is about.

\emph{Point masses.} $P_k = \delta_{m_k}$. Then $\bar A_{kl} = u(m_k, n_l)$: the
component matrix is the payoff table between the two players' locations, with no
approximation of any kind. If the locations are the equilibrium atoms, $\bar A$
\emph{is} the atom matrix $A_{jl} = u(p_j, r_l)$, and clause (ii) reads
$w_k\nabla Q_{\pi_{\theta_1}}(m_k)$ --- ascent on the raw landscape.

\emph{Diagonal Gaussians.} $P_k = \cN(m_k, \sigma_k^2)$, the implemented policy.
Then $\bar A_{kl} = A_{kl} + O(\sigma^2 + \varsigma^2)$, and clause (ii) reads
$w_k \nabla (Q_{\pi_{\theta_1}})_{\sigma_k}(m_k)$: ascent on the landscape blurred
at that component's own width, where $\Lambda_s := \Lambda * \cN(0,s^2 I)$.

The Gaussian family therefore does two things at once that the point-mass family
separates: it blurs the landscape being climbed, and it displaces the matrix being
played. Parts 2 and 4 will care only about the first, and only through the fact
that \emph{some} fixed landscape is being climbed.
\end{example}

\subsection*{1.2 Matrix games are solved}

\begin{lemma}[Tabular MMD on a fixed matrix; Sokota et al.\ 2023,
restated as \CV{Lemma~9}]\label{lem:mmd}
Fix a matrix $\bar A$. The categorical update run by both players has two nested
loops, and both converge:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{inner}: with the magnet snapshot held fixed, the iterates
  converge at a linear rate to the $\tau$-regularised equilibrium (the QRE) of
  $\bar A$;
\item[(ii)] \emph{outer}: refreshing the magnet to the current iterate is a
  proximal-point step, whose fixed points are exactly the Nash equilibria of
  $\bar A$ --- for \emph{every} $\tau > 0$ --- and which converges at a linear rate
  because the underlying variational inequality is polyhedral.
\end{enumerate}
There is no basin structure and no initialisation dependence in either loop.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
A zero-sum matrix game lifts to a monotone variational inequality; adding
entropy makes it strongly monotone, which gives (i). For (ii), at a fixed point
of the refresh map the magnet equals the iterate, so the regularisation term
vanishes identically and the first-order condition collapses to the plain
variational inequality of $\bar A$ --- which is why the regularisation biases the
\emph{rate} and not the \emph{limit}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why $\tau$ is not a bias knob]\label{rem:tau}
Part (ii) is worth pausing on, because it is easy to assume that entropy
regularisation must leave a residual $O(\tau)$ error. It does not, once the magnet
is refreshed: the refresh is what removes it. This will matter in Part 5, where
the composition wants $\tau$ \emph{large} for stability and one would otherwise
fear paying for it in accuracy.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{1.3 Part 1}

\begin{theorem}[The weights are free]\label{thm:part1}
Suppose player $0$'s component locations are held at the equilibrium atoms
$p_1,\dots,p_{M_0}$, and symmetrically for player $1$, with the component shapes
held fixed. Then the categorical heads converge to a Nash equilibrium of the
component matrix $\bar A$; the induced policy pair converges; and its
exploitability is exactly the \emph{spread deficit} of the component family,
\[
  \varepsilon \;=\; \Big[\max_\cA Q_\nu - \max_j \E_{a\sim P_j} Q_\nu(a)\Big]
  \;+\; \Big[\max_\cB W_\mu - \max_l \E_{b\sim R_l} W_\mu(b)\Big] .
\]
\emph{No hypothesis on the game, and none on the component family, is used.}
In the two instances of Example~\ref{ex:families}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{point masses}: $\bar A = A$ exactly, and $\varepsilon = 0$
  exactly --- the equilibrium is a fixed point of the algorithm, not a limit of
  one;
\item[(ii)] \emph{Gaussians at a floor $s^{\min}$}: $\bar A = A + O(s^2)$, the
  deficit is the blur deficit $\max_\cA Q_\nu - \max_\cA (Q_\nu)_{s^{\min}_0}$
  (and symmetrically), and
  $\varepsilon = O\big((s^{\min}_0)^2 + (s^{\min}_1)^2\big)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp} the heads play the matrix game $\bar A$, which is fixed
because the components are; by Lemma~\ref{lem:mmd} they converge to its Nash. For
the exploitability, the crucial observation is that a Nash of $\bar A$
\emph{equalises the components}: complementary slackness makes every component in
the support attain the same value, so the mixture's expected payoff equals
$\max_j \E_{P_j} Q_\nu$, and what is left is the gap between that and the best
achievable value of the landscape at a \emph{point}. For (i) the two are equal,
because an atom of a Nash equilibrium attains $\max_\cA Q_{\nu^\star}$ by the
support characterisation of a best response --- a theorem, not an assumption. For
(ii) the gap is a pure blur deficit, and Taylor-expanding the blur at the atom
gives the exponent $2$; this is where condition (B) --- $Q$ twice differentiable at
its atoms --- is used, and the only place in the entire development that it is.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The bias is a property of the family, not of the method]
\label{rem:biasfamily}
Clause (i) is worth stating even though the point-mass policy is not the one
implemented, because it isolates where the $O(s^2)$ of clause (ii) comes from.
It is not an artifact of mixing, of the matrix game, or of the algorithm: with
$K \ge M$ point masses the equilibrium is \emph{exactly representable} and the
bias is identically zero. It appears in clause (ii) for one reason only --- a
Gaussian of width $s \ge s^{\min} > 0$ is never a point mass, so the policy class
does not contain its target.

Part 3 is where that becomes a bargain rather than a defect: the same width that
causes the bias is what supplies the annealing mechanism, and the mechanism drives
the bias it caused to zero. But the accounting should be visible in advance ---
one family pays nothing here and gets nothing there.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[What Part 1 does \emph{not} say]\label{rem:part1not}
It says nothing about where the means are. Run the same argument with the means
in completely the wrong places and it still goes through --- the weights converge,
beautifully and globally, to the Nash of a matrix that describes the wrong
components. Part 1 is a statement about mixing, not about location.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Why a one-point condition can characterise a many-atom
equilibrium]\label{rem:atomnotnash}
Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp} is what makes the rest of the theory possible, and the
reason is worth stating explicitly because readers arriving from optimisation
stumble on it. A component mean is a \emph{point} of $\cA$, and no point of $\cA$
is a Nash equilibrium when $M_0 > 1$. So there can be no condition of the form
``each mean is attracted to the equilibrium''. What there can be is a condition of
the form ``each mean is attracted to \emph{one atom}'' --- and mixing over the
atoms is then not a geometric phenomenon at all, but a property of the weights,
which solve an ordinary matrix game. The problem factorises:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l p{0.32\textwidth} p{0.32\textwidth}@{}}
\hline
 & \emph{where are the atoms?} & \emph{how much mass on each?}\\
\hline
solved by & the component means & the categorical heads\\
mechanism & $M_i$ parallel single-target problems & one finite matrix game\\
needs & everything (Parts 2--5) & nothing (Part 1)\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{remark}

\part*{Part 2. On a fixed landscape: one attractor per cell}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Part 2}
\setcounter{section}{2}
\setcounter{theorem}{0}

\idea{Freeze the opponent. Then a component mean is doing gradient ascent on one
fixed function, and the question ``do the means find the atoms'' becomes the
purely single-agent question ``does gradient ascent find the peaks''. It has two
obstacles. There are several peaks and we want them all --- so we stop asking for
a global statement and partition the action set into one \emph{cell} per peak,
turning one $M$-target problem into $M$ single-target problems. And within a
cell, ascent can still stall --- so we ask for the weakest condition under which
it cannot: that the gradient, everywhere in the cell, has a positive component
pointing at the target. That is the whole hypothesis, and a one-line Lyapunov
argument shows it suffices. The other half of the part is that it cannot be
weakened: a cell with two attractors traps components in a way no hyperparameter
repairs.}

Throughout Part 2, fix an opponent policy $\nu$ and freeze the component shapes,
and write $\Lambda$ for the resulting fixed landscape on $\cA$ --- the function
whose gradient clause (ii) of Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp} delivers to a location
parameter. Only the locations move.

\begin{remark}[What Part 2 uses about $\Lambda$, and what it does not]
\label{rem:provenance}
Everything in this part is a statement about \emph{one fixed function on $\cA$ and
ascent on it}. The only properties of $\Lambda$ used anywhere below are that it is
differentiable with $L$-Lipschitz gradient, and that the update is
$m^+ = m + \eta\,P\,\nabla\Lambda(m)$ for some positive definite $P$ and a small
enough $\eta$. Where $\Lambda$ came from is never opened up.

This matters because it is the whole of the deferral promised in the overview.
Read $\Lambda = (Q_\nu)_s$, the blurred landscape of the Gaussian policy, and
Part 2 is the transport half of the implemented method. Read $\Lambda = Q_\nu$,
the raw landscape of a point-mass policy, and every statement below --- the
definition, the Lyapunov argument, the sharpness result, the counterexample ---
holds verbatim, with the raw landscape's own Lipschitz constant in place of the
blurred one's. The two readings diverge only in Part 3, and only because there
the width stops being frozen.

The preconditioner is likewise not used, and is likewise deferred. The results of
\S\ref{sec:transport} are stated for plain gradient ascent; the implemented step
carries a preconditioner built from the component's width, which is a Gaussian
object and so does not belong in a family-agnostic part. It is reinstated
separately in \S\ref{sec:precond}, where it turns out to rescale two constants and
change nothing else --- and Part 5's small-gain condition is invariant to it as
well (\S5.1, observation~2).
\end{remark}

\subsection*{2.1 Cells and attractors}

\begin{definition}[Attracting cell structure --- condition (C), frozen form]
\label{def:C}
$\Lambda$ has an \emph{$M$-cell attracting structure} if there are open sets
$\cA_1,\dots,\cA_M \subset \cA$ with disjoint closures, whose union has full
measure in $\cA$, each \emph{star-shaped about} a point $\xi_j \in \cA_j$ ---
meaning that for every $a \in \cA_j$ the whole segment
$\{\xi_j + t(a - \xi_j) : t \in [0,1]\}$ lies in $\cA_j$ --- such
that for every $j$ and every $a \in \cA_j$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:star}
  \ip{\nabla \Lambda(a)}{\xi_j - a}
  \;\ge\; \rho\big(\|\xi_j - a\|\big)\,\|\xi_j - a\| ,
\end{equation}
for some continuous $\rho$ with $\rho(0)=0$ and $\rho(r) > 0$ for $r > 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[What star-shapedness says, and why it is listed]
\label{rem:starshaped}
\emph{The condition.} ``$\cA_j$ is star-shaped about $\xi_j$'' says the target can
see the whole of its own cell: walk in a straight line from $\xi_j$ to any point
of $\cA_j$ and you never leave $\cA_j$. It is strictly weaker than convexity,
which asks the same of every pair of points rather than only of pairs containing
the centre --- a five-pointed star is star-shaped about its middle and not convex.
Concretely it forbids a cell that doubles back on itself, wraps around a hole, or
comes in several separated pieces.

\emph{What it is for.} Exactly one thing: Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}(iv) proves
maximality by integrating \eqref{eq:star} along the segment from $\xi_j$ to $a$,
and that argument needs the segment to lie where the hypothesis holds. Condition
\eqref{eq:star} says the gradient points along that segment; star-shapedness says
the segment is inside the cell. Neither implies the other, and the proof consumes
both.

\emph{Why it is not free.} It is tempting to drop it on the grounds that
\eqref{eq:star} ``makes the gradient point inward everywhere, so the cell must
already be star-shaped''. That inference is wrong, and the cheapest way to see it
is a disconnected cell. Let $\cA_j = B \cup B'$ where $B$ is a small ball about
$\xi_j$ and $B'$ is a far-away blob, separated from $B$ by territory belonging to
other cells. On $B'$ put $\Lambda(a) = c_0 - c\|a - \xi_j\|$, smoothed; then
$\ip{\nabla\Lambda(a)}{\xi_j - a} = c\|a-\xi_j\|$, so \eqref{eq:star} holds on
$B'$ for any $\rho \le c$. But $c_0$ is at our disposal --- $\Lambda$ is only
required to be continuous on $\cA$, and $B$ and $B'$ are not adjacent --- so take
$c_0$ large enough that $\Lambda > \Lambda(\xi_j)$ throughout $B'$. Then
\eqref{eq:star} holds on all of $\cA_j$ and $\xi_j$ is \emph{not} the maximiser
over $\overline{\cA_j}$. Star-shapedness rules this out, since a star-shaped set
is connected.

\emph{What is not settled.} Whether \eqref{eq:star} together with
\emph{connectedness} of $\cA_j$ already forces star-shapedness is left open here.
A natural attempt at a connected counterexample --- a slit annulus with $\Lambda$
increasing around it --- does not work: at the two lips of the slit the chord to
$\xi_j$ becomes orthogonal to the gradient, so $\ip{\nabla\Lambda}{\xi_j - a}
\to 0$ while $\|\xi_j - a\|$ stays bounded away from $0$, and \eqref{eq:star}
fails there for every admissible $\rho$. So the hypothesis may well be redundant
for connected cells; it is listed because the proof needs it and because it costs
nothing --- one must exhibit the cells and the targets anyway in order to claim a
game qualifies.

\emph{In one dimension it is automatic.} A connected open subset of $\R$ is an
interval, and an interval is star-shaped about each of its points. So the
condition has no content for the codebase's one-dimensional games; it bites only
in $n \ge 2$, and there it excludes only cells one would have to draw
deliberately.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Star-shapedness and the invariance radius are different
conditions]\label{rem:twoconditions}
Both were added to Part 2 at the same time and it is easy to run them together.
They are independent and do different jobs.

Star-shapedness is a property of the \emph{cell relative to its target}, it is
used only in Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}(iv), and it buys the static statement that
$\xi_j$ is where the maximum is. The invariance radius $R_j$ of \eqref{eq:Rj} is
a property of the \emph{initialisation relative to the cell}, it is used only in
Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}, and it buys the dynamic statement that a trajectory
cannot leak out of its cell.

Neither implies the other. A star-shaped cell can be a thin spike, so that a
point of it at distance $r$ from $\xi_j$ has $R_j \ll r$ and the trajectory
started there is not covered by Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}; conversely a large ball
around $\xi_j$ can sit inside a cell whose distant parts are not visible from
$\xi_j$ at all.
\end{remark}

Read \eqref{eq:star} literally: \emph{from anywhere in the cell, the gradient has
a positive component pointing at $\xi_j$}. It permits the gradient to be
arbitrarily small, to point mostly sideways, and the landscape to have flat
regions and inflections. What it forbids is exactly two things --- a second
attractor inside the cell, and the gradient pointing away from $\xi_j$ anywhere
in it.

\begin{remark}[$\xi_j$ is posited, not defined]\label{rem:posited}
The point $\xi_j$ is data of the hypothesis --- to claim a game qualifies you must
exhibit it --- not something defined as ``the critical point''. That it is a
critical point, and the unique one, is a consequence (Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}).
Setting it up this way collapses what would otherwise be three separate claims
(existence, uniqueness, attraction) into one checkable inequality, and it is why
(C) can be verified by counting modes rather than by solving for critical points.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[(C) is much weaker than concavity]\label{rem:weak}
A Gaussian bump $h\,e^{-x^2/2w^2}$ is concave only on $|x| \le w$ --- yet it
satisfies \eqref{eq:star} on its entire cell, because its gradient points uphill
everywhere. This matters practically: mixture-MMD converges on the codebase's
two-peak game ($w = 0.1$) from means far outside that radius, which no
concavity-based hypothesis could explain.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{2.2 One attractor per cell suffices}\label{sec:transport}

\paragraph{The update.} Throughout this subsection a location parameter runs
plain gradient ascent,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:update}
  m^+ \;=\; m \;+\; \eta\,\nabla\Lambda(m).
\end{equation}
The implemented algorithm does not: it carries a preconditioner, and for the
Gaussian policy that preconditioner is built from the component's own width. That
is a fact about the \emph{algorithm} and not about the game, it changes no
hypothesis and no conclusion, and putting it here would import the Gaussian into
a part that does not otherwise need it. So it is deferred to
\S\ref{sec:precond}, one subsection later, where it is discharged as a corollary
in half a page.

Assume $\nabla\Lambda$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $\cA_j$, and define the
\emph{invariance radius}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Rj}
  R_j \;:=\; \mathrm{dist}\big(\xi_j,\, \partial\cA_j\big) \;>\; 0,
\end{equation}
positive because $\cA_j$ is open and $\xi_j \in \cA_j$; equivalently, $R_j$ is the
largest $r$ with $\overline{B}(\xi_j, r) \subseteq \cA_j$.

\begin{lemma}[The target is critical, and the gradient is dominated;
\CV{Lemma~1}]\label{lem:crit}
Assume Definition~\ref{def:C}. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j) = 0$;
\item[(ii)] $\xi_j$ is the unique critical point of $\Lambda$ in $\cA_j$;
\item[(iii)] $\|\nabla\Lambda(a)\| \le L\,\|a - \xi_j\|$ for every
  $a \in \cA_j$;
\item[(iv)] $\xi_j$ is the unique maximiser of $\Lambda$ over
  $\overline{\cA_j}$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if \eqref{eq:star} holds with $\rho(r) \ge \alpha r$ then
$\alpha \le L$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(i) $\cA_j$ is open and $\xi_j \in \cA_j$, so $B(\xi_j,\epsilon_0) \subseteq
\cA_j$ for some $\epsilon_0 > 0$. Fix a unit vector $v$ and
$0 < \epsilon < \epsilon_0$, and put $a = \xi_j + \epsilon v \in \cA_j$. Then
$\xi_j - a = -\epsilon v$ and $\|\xi_j - a\| = \epsilon$, so \eqref{eq:star}
reads $\ip{\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j + \epsilon v)}{-\epsilon v} \ge \rho(\epsilon)
\epsilon$, that is,
\[
  \ip{\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j + \epsilon v)}{v} \;\le\; -\rho(\epsilon) \;<\; 0 .
\]
Let $\epsilon \downarrow 0$. Since $\nabla\Lambda$ is continuous and $\rho$ is
continuous with $\rho(0) = 0$, this gives $\ip{\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j)}{v} \le 0$
for \emph{every} unit vector $v$. If $\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j) \ne 0$, choosing
$v = \nabla\Lambda(\xi_j)/\|\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j)\|$ yields
$\|\nabla\Lambda(\xi_j)\| \le 0$, a contradiction.

(ii) If $a \in \cA_j$ with $a \ne \xi_j$ and $\nabla\Lambda(a) = 0$, then
\eqref{eq:star} at $a$ gives
$0 = \ip{0}{\xi_j - a} \ge \rho(\|\xi_j - a\|)\|\xi_j - a\| > 0$, since
$\|\xi_j - a\| > 0$ and $\rho$ is strictly positive off the origin.

(iii) By (i) and $L$-Lipschitzness,
$\|\nabla\Lambda(a)\| = \|\nabla\Lambda(a) - \nabla\Lambda(\xi_j)\|
\le L\|a - \xi_j\|$.

(iv) Let $a \in \overline{\cA_j}$, $a \ne \xi_j$, and write $r = \|a - \xi_j\|$.
Suppose first $a \in \cA_j$. By star-shapedness (Definition~\ref{def:C}) the
segment $a_t := \xi_j + t(a - \xi_j)$ lies in $\cA_j$ for $t \in (0,1]$. For such
$t$ we have $a - \xi_j = (a_t - \xi_j)/t$, so \eqref{eq:star} at $a_t$ gives
\[
  \ip{\nabla\Lambda(a_t)}{a - \xi_j}
  \;=\; -\tfrac{1}{t}\,\ip{\nabla\Lambda(a_t)}{\xi_j - a_t}
  \;\le\; -\tfrac{1}{t}\,\rho(tr)\,(tr) \;=\; -\rho(tr)\,r .
\]
Integrating along the segment,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:maximality}
  \Lambda(a) - \Lambda(\xi_j)
  \;=\; \int_0^1 \ip{\nabla\Lambda(a_t)}{a - \xi_j}\,dt
  \;\le\; -\,r\int_0^1 \rho(tr)\,dt \;<\; 0,
\end{equation}
the last inequality because $\rho > 0$ on $(0,r]$. For $a \in \partial\cA_j$,
apply \eqref{eq:maximality} to points of $\cA_j$ approaching $a$ and use
continuity of $\Lambda$, which gives $\Lambda(a) \le \Lambda(\xi_j)$; equality
would make $a$ a maximiser of $\Lambda$ over $\overline{\cA_j}$, and then
\eqref{eq:maximality} applied on the segment from $\xi_j$ towards $a$ is already
strict at every interior point, so $\Lambda(a) < \Lambda(\xi_j)$ after all.

Finally, combining (iii) with Cauchy--Schwarz and \eqref{eq:star},
$\alpha r^2 \le \ip{\nabla\Lambda(a)}{\xi_j - a} \le \|\nabla\Lambda(a)\|\,r
\le L r^2$, so $\alpha \le L$.
\end{proof}

Clause (iii) is small but is the reason everything later works: \emph{a
component's step shrinks as it arrives.} It is what separates ``converges'' from
``hovers within $O(\eta)$''. Clause (iv) is the only place star-shapedness is
used, and Remark~\ref{rem:starshaped} explains why it cannot be dispensed with.

\begin{theorem}[Transport on a fixed landscape]\label{thm:part2}
Assume Definition~\ref{def:C} and let $m(0) \in \cA_j$ satisfy
$r_0 := \|m(0) - \xi_j\| < R_j$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \emph{(continuous time)} the flow $\dot m = \nabla\Lambda(m)$ stays in
  $\overline{B}(\xi_j, r_0) \subseteq \cA_j$ for all $t \ge 0$,
  $\|m(t) - \xi_j\|$ is strictly decreasing, and $m(t) \to \xi_j$;
\item[(b)] \emph{(discrete time)} if in addition the modulus is linear,
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:Lpart2}
    \rho(r) \;\ge\; \alpha\,r \quad\text{on } (0, \mathrm{diam}\,\cA],
    \qquad \alpha > 0 \tag{L}
  \end{equation}
  and the step size satisfies
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:step2}
    0 \;<\; \eta \;<\; \frac{\alpha}{L^2},
  \end{equation}
  then the iterates \eqref{eq:update} stay in $\overline{B}(\xi_j, r_0)$ and
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:geo}
    \|m(t) - \xi_j\| \;\le\;
    \Big(1 - \tfrac12\,\eta\,\alpha\Big)^{t}\,\|m(0) - \xi_j\| .
  \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, if $K \ge M$ components are initialised so that every cell
contains at least one \emph{within its invariance radius}, then every cell's
attractor is found.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $d := m - \xi_j$, $G := \nabla\Lambda(m)$ and $V := \tfrac12\|d\|^2$.

(a) While $m \in \cA_j$, by \eqref{eq:star},
\[
  \dot V \;=\; \ip{d}{\nabla\Lambda(m)}
  \;=\; -\ip{\nabla\Lambda(m)}{\xi_j - m}
  \;\le\; -\rho(\|d\|)\,\|d\| \;<\; 0 \qquad (m \ne \xi_j).
\]
So $\|d\|$ is strictly decreasing and $m(t)$ remains in
$\overline{B}(\xi_j, r_0)$, which by $r_0 < R_j$ and \eqref{eq:Rj} lies in
$\cA_j$: the trajectory can never reach $\partial\cA_j$, so it never leaves, and
being confined to a compact set on which $\nabla\Lambda$ is Lipschitz it exists
for all $t \ge 0$. Now $V$ is nonincreasing and bounded below, so
$V(t) \downarrow V_\infty \ge 0$. If $V_\infty > 0$ then $\|d(t)\|$ lies in a
compact interval $[c, r_0]$ with $c > 0$ for all $t$, on which the continuous
function $r \mapsto \rho(r)r$ attains a positive minimum $\delta$; then
$\dot V \le -\delta$ for all $t$ and $V(t) \to -\infty$, a contradiction. Hence
$V_\infty = 0$, i.e.\ $m(t) \to \xi_j$.

(b) One step of \eqref{eq:update} gives $d^+ = d + \eta G$, so
\[
  V^+ \;=\; \tfrac12\|d + \eta G\|^2
  \;=\; V \;+\; \eta\ip{G}{d} \;+\; \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}\|G\|^2 .
\]
By \eqref{eq:star} and \eqref{eq:Lpart2}, $\ip{G}{d} = -\ip{G}{\xi_j - m} \le
-\rho(\|d\|)\|d\| \le -\alpha\|d\|^2$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}(iii),
$\|G\|^2 \le L^2\|d\|^2$. Since $\|d\|^2 = 2V$,
\[
  V^+ \;\le\; V \;-\; \eta\alpha\|d\|^2 \;+\; \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}L^2\|d\|^2
  \;=\; V\big(1 - 2\eta\alpha + \eta^2L^2\big)
  \;\le\; V\big(1 - \eta\alpha\big),
\]
the last step by \eqref{eq:step2}, which gives $\eta^2L^2 < \eta\alpha$. The
factor is nonnegative, since $\eta\alpha < \alpha^2/L^2 \le 1$ by
\eqref{eq:step2} and $\alpha \le L$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}. Taking square
roots and using $\sqrt{1-x} \le 1 - x/2$ on $[0,1]$ gives
$\|d^+\| \le (1 - \tfrac12\eta\alpha)\|d\|$. In particular $\|d\|$ never
increases, so the iterate remains in $\overline{B}(\xi_j,r_0) \subseteq \cA_j$
and the hypotheses hold again at $m^+$; \eqref{eq:geo} follows by induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Two hypotheses the sketch used silently]\label{rem:silent}
Both additions to the \emph{theorem} are forced, and each corresponds to
something \CV{} carries and this document had lost in compression. (The third
addition, star-shapedness, is to the \emph{definition} and does a different job;
Remark~\ref{rem:twoconditions} separates them.)

\emph{The ball, not the cell.} ``Starts in $\cA_j$'' is not enough for
invariance. What the Lyapunov argument delivers is that the \emph{distance} to
$\xi_j$ does not grow, and that confines the trajectory to $\cA_j$ only if the
ball of that radius is inside $\cA_j$ --- which is $r_0 < R_j$. Without it a
component can exit through a nearby piece of $\partial\cA_j$ while moving
strictly closer to $\xi_j$, land in a neighbouring cell, and converge to the
wrong attractor. This is exactly the quantitative reading of the covering clause
that \CV{(G$_\sigma$)} imposes on the initialisation, and it is why the last
sentence of Theorem~\ref{thm:part2} says ``within its invariance radius'' rather
than merely ``in the cell''.

\emph{(L), three parts early.} The discrete iteration needs a \emph{linear}
modulus, and this is not a matter of rate. See Remark~\ref{rem:needL}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Why (L) cannot be postponed]\label{rem:needL}
It is natural to read \eqref{eq:Lpart2} as buying a rate --- geometric decay
rather than mere monotonicity --- and that is how Part 5 will present it
(Remark~\ref{rem:L}). In discrete time with a \emph{fixed} step it buys more than
that: it buys convergence.

The reason is visible in the proof. The step $\eta G$ splits into a component
along $d$ and a component across it, worth respectively
\[
  \eta\,\ip{G}{d} \;\le\; -\eta\,\rho(\|d\|)\,\|d\|
  \qquad\text{and}\qquad
  \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}\|G\|^2 \;\le\; \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}L^2\|d\|^2 .
\]
Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}(iii) makes the second term
quadratic in $\|d\|$ --- that is the part the sketch was right about --- but the
first is quadratic only if $\rho(r)/r$ is bounded below. For a modulus vanishing
faster than linearly, say $\rho(r) = r^{1+\delta}$ with $\delta > 0$, the estimate
reads
\[
  V^+ \;\le\; V \;-\; \eta\|d\|^{2+\delta}
  \;+\; \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}L^2\|d\|^2 ,
\]
whose negative term dominates only while
$\|d\|^{\delta} > \tfrac{\eta}{2}L^2$. Below that radius a fixed step size
certifies nothing, and the tangential drift is genuinely unpaid rather than
merely unaccounted for: it is second order in $\eta$ but only second order in
$\|d\|$, so nothing but a radial gain that is also second order in $\|d\|$ can
absorb it --- which is \eqref{eq:Lpart2}.

So the honest division is: the flow converges under (C) alone, the fixed-step
iteration needs (C) and (L), and a diminishing step size $\eta_t \to 0$ with
$\sum_t \eta_t = \infty$ recovers convergence under (C) alone at the cost of the
geometric rate. Since Part 2 is about the algorithm rather than about a flow,
(L) is a Part 2 hypothesis; Part 5 does not introduce it, it only names it and
notices that the same constant is what the scale block needs.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[What ``small $\rho$'' costs]\label{rem:rho}
Nothing in \eqref{eq:Lpart2} requires $\alpha$ to be \emph{large}, and it
typically is not: for a Gaussian bump of width $w$, $\rho(r) \asymp (h/w^2)\,r\,
e^{-r^2/2w^2}$, so $\alpha \asymp (h/w^2)e^{-\mathrm{diam}^2/2w^2}$ ---
exponentially small, the ``dead zone'' where the landscape is essentially flat.
Theorem~\ref{thm:part2} is uniform in this: \eqref{eq:geo} holds for every
$\alpha > 0$ and only the rate degrades. This demotes the previous theory's
$s_{\mathrm{reach}}$ from a hypothesis to a \emph{rate}, and it matches the
observation that borderline configurations converge at $150$k steps and not at
$40$k.

The distinction that matters is therefore not between large and small $\alpha$,
but between $\rho$ decaying at long range --- harmless, and the generic case ---
and $\rho$ vanishing superlinearly \emph{at the target}, which is what
Remark~\ref{rem:needL} excludes. A nondegenerate maximum never does the latter,
which is why (L) costs so little in practice.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{2.3 Preconditioning moves the constants and nothing else}
\label{sec:precond}

The implemented algorithm does not run \eqref{eq:update}. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp}(ii) it runs a \emph{preconditioned} step,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:updateP}
  m^+ \;=\; m \;+\; \eta\,P\,\nabla\Lambda(m),
\end{equation}
with $P$ symmetric positive definite. This subsection discharges the difference.
It is short on purpose: the entire content is that $P$ rescales two constants and
touches nothing else.

\begin{corollary}[Transport under a preconditioner]\label{cor:precond}
Let $P$ be symmetric positive definite with spectrum in $[p_-, p_+]$,
$0 < p_- \le p_+$, and put $\|x\|_{P^{-1}} := (x^\top P^{-1}x)^{1/2}$, so that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:normeq}
  \|x\|/\sqrt{p_+} \;\le\; \|x\|_{P^{-1}} \;\le\; \|x\|/\sqrt{p_-} .
\end{equation}
Define $R_j^P$ as the largest $r$ with
$\{a : \|a - \xi_j\|_{P^{-1}} \le r\} \subseteq \cA_j$. Then
Theorem~\ref{thm:part2} holds verbatim for \eqref{eq:updateP} with
$\|\cdot\|$ replaced by $\|\cdot\|_{P^{-1}}$ and $R_j$ by $R_j^P$, on replacing
the step-size bound and the rate by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stepP}
  0 \;<\; \eta \;<\; \frac{\alpha}{p_+ L^2},
  \qquad\text{rate}\quad 1 - \tfrac12\,\eta\,\alpha\,p_- .
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Take $V := \tfrac12\|d\|^2_{P^{-1}}$ in place of $\tfrac12\|d\|^2$. In continuous
time, $\dot V = \ip{d}{P^{-1}P\nabla\Lambda(m)} = \ip{\nabla\Lambda(m)}{d}$ ---
the preconditioner cancels exactly --- and the rest of part~(a) is unchanged. In
discrete time, $d^+ = d + \eta PG$ gives
\[
  V^+ \;=\; V \;+\; \eta\ip{d}{PG}_{P^{-1}}
  \;+\; \tfrac{\eta^2}{2}\|PG\|^2_{P^{-1}},
\]
and again $P$ cancels in the cross term, $\ip{d}{PG}_{P^{-1}} = d^\top P^{-1}PG
= \ip{d}{G}$, while $\|PG\|^2_{P^{-1}} = G^\top PG \le p_+\|G\|^2$. Bounding
$\ip{G}{d} \le -\alpha\|d\|^2$ and $\|G\|^2 \le L^2\|d\|^2$ as before,
\[
  V^+ \;\le\; V - \eta\|d\|^2\Big(\alpha - \tfrac{\eta}{2}p_+L^2\Big)
  \;\le\; V - \tfrac{\eta\alpha}{2}\|d\|^2 ,
\]
by \eqref{eq:stepP}. Finally $\|d\|^2 \ge p_-\|d\|^2_{P^{-1}} = 2p_-V$ by
\eqref{eq:normeq}, so $V^+ \le V(1 - \eta\alpha p_-)$, whose factor is
nonnegative because $\eta\alpha p_- < \alpha^2 p_-/(p_+L^2) \le \alpha^2/L^2
\le 1$. Square roots as before.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[$P$ is a property of the algorithm, not of the game]
\label{rem:Palg}
The division is clean and worth stating once, because it recurs in every later
part. The \emph{game} supplies $\Lambda$ --- its cells, its targets $\xi_j$, the
modulus $\rho$ and hence $\alpha$, the Lipschitz constant $L$. The
\emph{algorithm} supplies $P$ and $\eta$. No hypothesis of
Definition~\ref{def:C}, and no constant in it, mentions $P$.

Three consequences, all visible in \eqref{eq:stepP}. First, $P$ does not move the
limit: $m + \eta P\nabla\Lambda(m) = m$ if and only if $\nabla\Lambda(m) = 0$, for
any invertible $P$, so preconditioning changes the path and the rate and never
the destination. Second, the \emph{only} thing the proof asks of $P$ is
$p_- > 0$, uniformly in time --- a preconditioner that degenerates along the
trajectory stalls the component wherever it happens to be, which is not a
statement about the landscape at all. Third, an anisotropic $P$ makes the
invariant sets ellipsoids rather than balls; the resulting condition number
$p_+/p_-$ is what \CV{} carries as $\chi^2$, and it is the one place a
preconditioner leaves a fingerprint on a conclusion rather than on a constant.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Which $P$, and why it is deferred]\label{rem:whichP}
For a point-mass policy $P = I$ and Corollary~\ref{cor:precond} is vacuous. For
the Gaussian policy of Part 3 it is not: Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp}(ii) delivers
$P = \sigma_k^2 w_k I$, the $\sigma_k^2$ being the inverse Fisher metric of a
Gaussian in its mean --- so the width of a component sets how fast its mean
moves. That is a genuinely Gaussian object, which is why it is quarantined here
rather than run through \S\ref{sec:transport}, and it is where two later
discussions come from: Remark~\ref{rem:throttle}, on what happens to $p_-$ when
the widths collapse, and observation~2 of \S5.1, on why $P$ cancels out of the
stability condition entirely.

The factor $w_k$ is a separate matter and is not natural-gradient at all. The
Fisher metric of the joint law $p(k,a) = w_k\,\cN(a \mid m_k,\sigma_k^2)$ has
$m_k$-block $w_k/\sigma_k^2$, so the exact natural gradient is
$\sigma_k^2\nabla\Lambda(m_k)$ with \emph{no} $w_k$; the implemented
$\sigma_k^2$ is the inverse Fisher metric of the \emph{conditional} law only. The
discrepancy is the whole of the weight-starvation freeze --- a component losing
weight also loses mobility, i.e.\ $p_- \to 0$ in Remark~\ref{rem:Palg}, and
freezes at a non-atom. It is carried in \GC{} as hypothesis (W), a floor
$w_k \ge w_{\min} > 0$, and that hypothesis becomes \emph{vacuous} under the
corrected preconditioner $\sigma_k^2/w_k$. So the one place where $P$ can hurt is
a fixable defect in the implementation rather than a property of any game.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{2.4 It cannot be weakened}

Definition~\ref{def:C} looks like a convenient hypothesis. It is a forced one.

\begin{proposition}[A second attractor is fatal]\label{prop:sharp}
Suppose a cell contains a second nondegenerate local maximum $\hat a \ne \xi_j$.
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\hat a$ is an exponentially stable fixed point of the mean dynamics,
  with an \emph{open} set of initialisations converging to it;
\item[(ii)] it is \textbf{magnet-invariant}: the magnet snapshot is refreshed to
  the trap itself, so the magnet's gradient vanishes there identically --- and the
  magnet adds $-\eta\tau I$ to the Jacobian, strictly \emph{deepening} the trap;
\item[(iii)] it is \textbf{capacity-invariant}: spare components initialised in
  its basin are captured too, so raising $K$ does not help;
\item[(iv)] it is \textbf{annealing-invariant}, and worse: an annealing schedule
  is the mechanism that \emph{delivers} components into it, since which branch a
  component follows down the scale ladder is decided at large blur.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
(i) is elementary: local ascent near a nondegenerate maximum is a contraction, and
contraction is an open condition. (ii)--(iv) are the substantive claims and each
is a one-line computation: for (ii), at the trap $\bar a = \hat a$, so the
quadratic term $-\tau(\hat a - \bar a)/\bar\sigma^2$ is identically zero, and
$-\eta\tau I$ is negative definite. Full statements in \GC{Proposition~1} and
\GC{Proposition~4}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The trap does not depend on the component family either]
\label{rem:trapfamily}
Clause (ii) is stated for the implemented Gaussian magnet, whose mean-dependent
part is the quadratic $-\tfrac{\tau}{2}\|a-\bar a\|^2_{\bar\sigma^{-2}}$, but the
computation touches nothing else: any proximal term of that shape, KL-derived or
imposed by hand, gives the same vanishing gradient at the trap and the same
$-\eta\tau I$. So (ii) survives a point-mass policy, where the divergence between
two components is not a usable quantity but a proximal penalty still is.

Clause (iv) is the one exception in this part, and it is vacuous rather than
false: with no width there is nothing to anneal, so there is no schedule to be
invariant to. It is stated here because the schedule \emph{is} present in Part 3,
and the reader should meet the objection in the part that raises it.
\end{remark}

The consequence is the shape of the whole theory: \emph{any class of games that
is robust to initialisation must forbid spurious attractors outright.} Not merely
dominated ones --- the previous theory's ``every spurious bump is dominated in
height and mass by the atoms'' controls which branch is the \emph{global} maximum,
but local ascent never compares global amplitudes, and a dominated spurious bump
still captures whatever enters it. Condition (C) is derived, not chosen.

\begin{example}[The decoy game, excluded by counting]\label{ex:decoy}
The codebase's decoy game has a landscape with three local maxima while its
equilibrium has support size two. Definition~\ref{def:C} asks for exactly $M = 2$
cells each containing one attractor, which is impossible: some cell contains two.
By Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp} there is an open set of initialisations from which
the method does not converge to Nash --- which is precisely what is observed. No
dominance computation, no smoothing analysis: a mode count against a support
size.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[This is also the certificate --- and it is the one place in this
part where the kernel matters]\label{rem:check}
Example~\ref{ex:decoy} is the cheapest useful thing in the theory. Definition
\ref{def:C} has to hold not at one width but across the whole admissible range,
which looks like an infinite family of checks. In one dimension it collapses to
two, by \emph{scale-space causality}: Gaussian blurring never creates a local
extremum, so the mode count of $\Lambda_s$ is non-increasing in $s$, and it
suffices to count modes at the \emph{bottom} of the range and check that no two
merge before the top --- two one-dimensional root counts, computable before
training. Structure finer than the smallest width is invisible and therefore
free, which is why the condition tolerates ripple, discretisation noise, or a
Monte-Carlo estimate of the landscape.

This is the exception to Remark~\ref{rem:provenance}, and it should be flagged
rather than buried. Causality is not a generic property of blurring: in one
dimension the Gaussian is essentially the only kernel with it, and in two or more
it fails even for the Gaussian. So the \emph{theorems} of this part hold for any
component family, but this \emph{certificate} for their hypothesis is Gaussian
and one-dimensional.

Note carefully which Gaussian is being used. It is the one smoothing the
landscape, not the one parameterising the policy --- $\Lambda_s$ is a blur of $Q_\nu$,
and the certificate cares only that the blur is Gaussian. A point-mass policy that
obtains its gradients from a Gaussian exploration kernel of bandwidth $h$ keeps the
certificate intact, with $h$ in place of $s$; one that uses a uniform ball, an
antithetic pair, or a finite difference does not.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{2.5 Is (C) checkable?}

A hypothesis that cannot be checked is a hypothesis that has been assumed away, so
this deserves a direct answer. It is: \emph{partially, by three routes, and the
limits of each are known.}

The first thing to see is that Definition~\ref{def:C} as stated in this part is
the \emph{easy} case, because freezing the opponent and the width hid two
quantifiers. The condition the later parts actually need is
\eqref{eq:star} holding
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt]
\item for every width $s$ in the admissible range $\Sigma_0$ (Part 3 unfreezes
  it), and
\item for every opponent policy $\nu$ the run can visit (Part 4 unfreezes it),
  \textbf{with the same cells} --- for the reason given in
  Remark~\ref{rem:fixed}.
\end{itemize}
Neither quantifier can be checked by evaluating anything finitely many times. Each
is collapsed by a separate structural assumption, and by nothing else.

\paragraph{The width quantifier: mode counting.} This is
Remark~\ref{rem:check}, and it is the cheap one. In one dimension Gaussian
blurring never creates a local extremum, so ``exactly $M_0$ attractors at every
width in $\Sigma_0$'' reduces to a count at the bottom of the range plus a check
that no two merge before the top: two one-dimensional root counts.

\paragraph{The opponent quantifier: finite-rank coupling.} Suppose the payoff has
the form that Part 4 will use anyway,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:finiterank}
  u(a,b) \;=\; D_0(a) - D_1(b) + \ip{\varphi(a)}{C\,\psi(b)},
  \qquad \varphi : \cA \to \R^{m_0},\ \psi : \cB \to \R^{m_1} .
\end{equation}
Then $Q_\nu(a) = D_0(a) + \ip{\varphi(a)}{Cy} + \mathrm{const}$ with
$y = \E_\nu \psi$: \emph{the opponent enters through $m_1$ numbers and nothing
else}. The quantifier over all probability measures on $\cB$ collapses to a
quantifier over the compact convex \emph{moment body}
$Y := \conv \psi(\cB) \subset \R^{m_1}$. An infinite-dimensional condition becomes
a finite-dimensional one, and for small $m_1$ a griddable one.

\paragraph{And then removing it: a closed-form certificate.} Better than gridding,
the same structure discharges the quantifier outright.

\begin{proposition}[Curvature dominates coupling, for (C); \GC{Proposition~2}]
\label{prop:capC}
Assume \eqref{eq:finiterank}. Suppose the \emph{self-term alone} satisfies
Definition~\ref{def:C}: $D_{0,s}$ is star-concave toward a branch
$\xi^0_j(s)$ on $\cA_j$, with modulus $\rho_0$. If
\begin{equation}\label{eq:capC}
  \sup_{y \in Y}\|Cy\| \cdot \sup_{\cA_j}\|\nabla\varphi_s\|
  \;<\; \inf_{r > 0} \rho_0(r),
\end{equation}
then \eqref{eq:star} holds for the \emph{coupled} landscape $(Q_\nu)_s$, for every
$\nu$, with modulus $\rho_0 - \|Cy\|\,\|\nabla\varphi_s\|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
The coupling perturbs the gradient by $\nabla\varphi_s^\top Cy$, whose norm is at
most the left-hand side of \eqref{eq:capC}; a perturbation smaller than the
modulus cannot reverse the sign of an inner product bounded below by that modulus.
The cells and the star-concavity survive; only $\rho$ shrinks.
\end{proof}

In words: \emph{check your own landscape, then check the opponent cannot bend it
enough to matter.} The first half is a single-agent, opponent-free computation ---
for a sum of Gaussian bumps it is the mode count above. The second half is one
inequality between norms.

\begin{remark}[The check certifies more than this part needs]\label{rem:aheadofC}
Proposition~\ref{prop:capC} proves \eqref{eq:star} for \emph{every} $\nu$, which is
strictly stronger than Definition~\ref{def:C}, whose $\nu$ was frozen. That is
deliberate and not an oversight of ordering: nobody would ever verify the frozen
version, because a frozen opponent is not a situation the algorithm is ever in.
The condition one checks in practice is the uniform one, and it is checked in one
step rather than assembled from Part 2 and repaired in Part 4. Part 2 states the
frozen form only because it is the form in which the Lyapunov argument is legible.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[How conservative \eqref{eq:capC} is]\label{rem:conservative}
Very, and in a predictable direction. The right-hand side is an infimum of $\rho_0$
over the \emph{whole} cell, and by Remark~\ref{rem:rho} that infimum is attained
far from the target where the landscape is nearly flat --- for a Gaussian bump of
width $w$ it is $O(e^{-\mathrm{diam}^2/2w^2})$, exponentially small. So
\eqref{eq:capC} demands that the coupling be weaker than the \emph{weakest} pull
anywhere in the cell, and a game can satisfy (C) comfortably while failing
\eqref{eq:capC} by orders of magnitude.

This should be read as what it is: a sufficient condition that is cheap and
crude, not a characterisation. It is worth distinguishing from the superficially
similar cap of Proposition~\ref{prop:cap} in Part 4, which governs the
\emph{small-gain} condition rather than (C), is stated in terms of the curvature
$\alpha$ at the branch rather than $\inf\rho_0$ over the cell, and is the one that
holds with three orders of margin on the codebase's games. The two are different
conditions with different constants and should not be conflated because they share
a name.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{The cheap tests are the useful ones.} In practice one rarely certifies
(C); one tries to \emph{refute} it, and two one-line tests do most of the work.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Mode count against support size.} (C) with (M) asks for exactly
  $M_0$ attractors. Count the local maxima of the landscape at the floor width; if
  the count exceeds $|\supp\mu^\star|$, the game is out, and by
  Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp} it is out for good rather than out pending better
  hyperparameters. This is Example~\ref{ex:decoy} in one sentence, and it is the
  cheapest useful thing in the theory.
\item \textbf{Resolution at the floor.} (C) provides $M_0$ distinct branch points
  at every admissible width, in particular the smallest, and (M) identifies them
  with the atoms there --- so the atoms must be \emph{resolvable} at the floor. For
  a landscape of equal Gaussian bumps of width $\bar w$ this is the closed-form
  inequality
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:resolution}
    \sqrt{\bar w^2 + (s^{\min})^2} \;<\; \tfrac12 \min_{j\ne j'}\|p_j - p_{j'}\| ,
  \end{equation}
  from the classical bimodality threshold for an equal-weight Gaussian pair. Read
  in the other direction it constrains a \emph{hyperparameter} rather than the
  game: \emph{do not choose a floor larger than half the atom spacing}. On the
  two-peak game ($\bar w = 0.1$, $s^{\min} = 10^{-3}$, spacing $1$) the left side
  is $0.1000005$ against $0.5$ --- so it is the bump width, not the floor, that
  binds, and it holds with a factor $5$ to spare.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Where this stops working.} Three limits, stated plainly.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Dimension kills the mode count.} For $n \ge 2$, Gaussian smoothing
  \emph{can} create local maxima, and a sum of three or more Gaussians can have
  more modes than components. Scale-space causality is a one-dimensional theorem.
  Above one dimension (C) must be imposed on the entire width range and the honest
  certificate is a numerical mode-ladder check, not a proof.
\item \textbf{There is no third route.} The opponent quantifier is collapsed by a
  finite-rank coupling or by one-dimensional causality, and --- as \CV{\S8} puts it
  --- \emph{not otherwise}. A payoff with no finite-rank structure, in dimension
  above one, leaves (C) as an assumption with no certificate attached.
\item \textbf{The self-term still has to be checked.} Proposition~\ref{prop:capC}
  reduces (C) for the game to (C) for $D_0$, which is progress only because $D_0$
  is opponent-free. For a $D_0$ available only as a black box or an estimate,
  ``count its modes'' is itself a numerical question --- though a benign one, since
  by Remark~\ref{rem:floor} structure finer than the floor is invisible and
  therefore free.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{2.6 What Part 2 leaves open}

Three things, each handled by a later part, and it is worth being explicit about
them because each is a place a reader could reasonably object.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{The peaks found are not yet the Nash atoms.} With the opponent
  frozen at an arbitrary $\nu$, Theorem~\ref{thm:part2} delivers the maxima of
  $(Q_\nu)_s$ --- best responses to $\nu$, not equilibrium atoms. They coincide
  only when $\nu = \nu^\star$. Condition (M) will assert exactly that the branch
  endpoints at the equilibrium opponent are the atoms; making the opponent
  actually get there is Part 4.
\item \textbf{The blur width was frozen, and the algorithm does not freeze it.}
  The target $\xi_j$ depends on $s$; as $s$ changes the target moves. Part 3
  shows the scales fall on their own, and that the target walks onto the true peak
  as they do.
\item \textbf{Exploration between cells is not solved, and cannot be.} Local
  ascent cannot move a component from one cell to another; which attractor a
  component finds is decided at initialisation. This is a genuine limitation of
  the algorithm, not an artifact of the analysis, and the honest response is to
  assume covering (every cell gets a component) and note that overcapacity with a
  spread initialisation buys it cheaply --- \emph{inside} the class, where by
  Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp} there is no spurious attractor for the spare
  components to fall into.
\item \textbf{The gradient was assumed, not obtained.} Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}
  applies $\nabla\Lambda$; it does not say where that comes from. The assumption is
  an exact deterministic oracle, and it is carried unchanged through Part 5, where
  it is listed as the largest remaining gap (\S5.4, item~1). It is worth
  separating here from the previous item, because the word ``exploration'' does
  duty for both and they are not the same problem: that one is about crossing
  between cells, this one is about getting a gradient at all.

  This is also the one place where the reading of Remark~\ref{rem:provenance} is
  not free. Under the Gaussian reading the component genuinely samples a
  neighbourhood, and $\nabla(Q_\nu)_{\sigma}(m)$ is an expectation under the
  policy itself, estimable on-policy; the oracle is an idealisation of something
  that exists. Under the point-mass reading there is no on-policy gradient to
  idealise --- the estimate must come from perturbations at some exploration
  bandwidth, off-policy --- and its noise does \emph{not} shrink as the component
  arrives, which is exactly the property Lemma~\ref{lem:crit} supplies and
  everything after it consumes. The theorems are the same; the distance from the
  theorems to an implementation is not.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{2.7 Where Part 2 goes}

Theorem~\ref{thm:part2} is not superseded later; it is \emph{invoked}. The
statement that survives into the full proof is its discrete-time form, part~(b),
in the preconditioned dress of Corollary~\ref{cor:precond} (\CV{Lemma~4}): one
step of ascent contracts the distance to a \emph{frozen} target by a definite
factor. Part 3 and Part 4 then add exactly one term to it,
\[
  \underbrace{\text{distance to target}}_{\text{shrinks, Part 2}}
  \;+\;
  \underbrace{\text{how far the target moved}}_{\text{Parts 3 and 4}},
\]
where the target moves because the blur width shrank (Part 3) or because the
opponent did something (Part 4). The cells do not move, and the Lyapunov function
$V = \tfrac12\|a-\xi_j\|^2$ does not change --- nor does it when the
preconditioner is put back, since by Corollary~\ref{cor:precond} that only
re-measures it. Everything after Part 2 is an argument that the second term
eventually stops mattering.

\part*{Part 3. The blur anneals itself, and sets the bias}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Part 3}
\setcounter{section}{3}
\setcounter{theorem}{0}

\idea{Part 2 froze the blur width. The algorithm does not: each component's width
is a parameter with its own update, so the landscape a component climbs is a
blurred version of the real one and the blur is moving. Two things happen, and
both are good. The blur collapses \emph{on its own} --- nobody schedules it ---
because at a peak, blurring costs you value, so the update pushes the width down.
And as it collapses, the target walks onto the true peak. What is left over is a
pure blur deficit, and that deficit is exactly the exploitability. This is the
part of the theory that makes parameter-free numerical predictions, and they hold
to four digits. It is also the part that has to choose a component family, and
the choice is not incidental: the mechanism \emph{is} a property of the Gaussian.}

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{This is where the Gaussian is bought.} Parts 1 and 2 were stated for an
arbitrary component family and used nothing about it: Part 1 needed only that the
per-component expectations exist, Part 2 only that some fixed differentiable
landscape is being climbed (Remark~\ref{rem:provenance}). From here to the end of
this part, the components are diagonal Gaussians, and two properties of that
choice are used essentially --- neither of which any other family supplies:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{The width is a parameter with its own gradient.} It is not a
  hyperparameter and not a schedule; it is a coordinate of the policy, and the
  same objective that moves the means moves it. A point-mass policy has no such
  coordinate, and a policy whose gradients come from a fixed exploration bandwidth
  has one that is exogenous --- something to be tuned, not something that moves.
\item \textbf{The Gaussian is the heat kernel.} Blurring at width $s$ is running
  the heat equation for time $s^2/2$. This is what turns ``what does the width
  update do?'' into ``what is the sign of the Laplacian at a peak?'', which has an
  answer. It is a property of the Gaussian specifically, not of blurring: no other
  family gives Lemma~\ref{lem:heat}.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
What is bought with them is the whole of this part --- annealing with no schedule,
a bias with a closed form, and the theory's only four-digit predictions. What is
paid is the $O(s^2)$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:part1}(ii), which
Remark~\ref{rem:biasfamily} already isolated as the price of leaving the
point-mass family. The bargain is that the mechanism drives its own price to zero:
Corollary~\ref{cor:unfl}.

Fix the opponent, as in Part 2, but now let both the means and the widths move.
Write $\Lambda_s := \Lambda * \cN(0, s^2 I)$ for the landscape blurred at width
$s$, and let $\xi_j(s)$ be the attractor of cell $j$ at that width --- a curve, the
\emph{scale-space branch}, which is where the name comes from.

\subsection*{3.1 Why the width falls by itself}

\begin{lemma}[Blurring costs value at a peak; \GC{Lemma~3}]\label{lem:heat}
For any $\Lambda$ and any width $s$,
\[
  \frac{\partial}{\partial (s^2/2)}\,\Lambda_s \;=\; \Delta \Lambda_s ,
\]
the heat equation. At an interior maximum of $\Lambda_s$ the Laplacian is
non-positive, and strictly negative if the maximum is nondegenerate. Hence the
log-width update of the algorithm is strictly negative near a branch point, and
the width decreases.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Blurring is running the heat equation for time $s^2/2$; the derivative of the
value at the current peak is the Laplacian there, the $\dot\xi$ term dropping out
by the envelope theorem. A peak is locally concave, so its Laplacian is negative:
smoothing a peak lowers it.
\end{proof}

This replaces the codebase's empirical ``self-annealing'' with a proof, and it
holds in any dimension. It also explains why an explicit annealing schedule is
never needed to get $\sigma \to 0$: a schedule only changes \emph{which} branch a
component follows on the way down --- which, as Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp}(iv)
noted, is precisely the mechanism by which annealing delivers components into a
decoy in games outside the class.

Lemma~\ref{lem:heat} is the exact point at which this document stops being
family-agnostic, and the dependence is not incidental but definitional: the
identity is the Gaussian semigroup's, and a family without it has no reason for
its spread to fall at a peak rather than rise. Every subsequent statement in this
part --- the arrival time, the branch displacement, the bias law, the magnet's
brake factor --- is downstream of this one line.

\begin{lemma}[Uniform version, giving a finite arrival time;
\CV{Lemma~7}]\label{lem:arrive}
The conclusion of Lemma~\ref{lem:heat} holds not only \emph{at} the branch point
but throughout a ball of radius $\delta^\kappa$ around it, uniformly. Hence once a
component has \emph{approximately} arrived, its width falls at a definite rate and
reaches the floor $s^{\min}$ in finitely many steps --- after which it is constant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Nondegeneracy at the branch point gives $\Delta\Lambda_s(\xi_j) \le -\kappa < 0$;
the third derivative of $\Lambda_s$ is bounded, so the Laplacian is still below
$-\kappa/2$ within a ball of radius $\kappa/(2L^{(3)})$.
\end{proof}

The upgrade from ``at the peak'' to ``near the peak'' is what makes this usable:
transport and annealing happen simultaneously, so one never gets to apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:heat} at an exactly arrived component. The finite arrival time
matters too --- after it, the width contributes \emph{exactly zero} disturbance to
the means, which is what lets the final argument conclude convergence rather than
convergence-to-a-ball.

\subsection*{3.2 The target walks onto the atom}

\begin{lemma}[The branch moves slowly in the right variable;
\CV{Lemma~3}]\label{lem:branch}
$\|\partial \xi_j / \partial (s^2/2)\| \le L^{(3)}/\alpha$: the branch point moves
by $O(1)$ per unit of $s^2/2$. Since the algorithm updates $\log s$, the
displacement of the target caused by one width step carries a factor $s^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Implicit function theorem applied to $\nabla \Lambda_s(\xi_j(s)) = 0$, using the
heat equation for the $s$-derivative and nondegeneracy to invert the Hessian.
\end{proof}

This is a small statement with an outsized consequence, and it is worth spelling
out because it answers the most natural objection to the whole scheme.

\begin{remark}[``Doesn't the collapse freeze transport?'']\label{rem:throttle}
It looks as though it must. The mean update is preconditioned by $\sigma^2$, so
between $\sigma = 0.1$ and $\sigma = 10^{-3}$ the mean's contraction per step
falls by four orders of magnitude: a component that has not arrived before the
width collapses looks like it should be frozen where it stands. But the
disturbance the collapse injects falls by \emph{six} orders --- one factor $s^2$
from Lemma~\ref{lem:branch} and one from the preconditioner in the width update
itself. Only the \emph{ratio} matters, and the ratio improves as the widths fall.
Transport is not throttled relative to what it is chasing; both slow down
together, and the disturbance slows faster (\CV{Lemma~8}).
\end{remark}

\subsection*{3.3 Part 3, and the numbers}

\begin{theorem}[Transport and annealing, fixed opponent]\label{thm:part3}
Assume Definition~\ref{def:C} holds at \emph{every} width in the admissible range
$[s^{\min}, s^{\max}]$, with nondegenerate branch points. Then, with the opponent
fixed, every component mean converges to the branch point of its cell, every
width falls to the floor in finite time, and the residual exploitability is the
blur deficit,
\[
  \varepsilon \;=\; \max_\cA \Lambda - \max_\cA \Lambda_{s^{\min}}
  \;=\; O\big((s^{\min})^2\big),
\]
with the exponent $2$ exactly when the landscape is twice differentiable at its
peaks.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Part 2 gives the means converging for each frozen width; Lemma~\ref{lem:arrive}
gives the widths falling once the means are approximately arrived;
Remark~\ref{rem:throttle} is what lets the two run simultaneously rather than in
sequence. The bias is Theorem~\ref{thm:part1}'s computation at the floor.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Release the floor: $\varepsilon = \Theta(1/t)$;
\GC{Corollary~2}]\label{cor:unfl}
If the floor is removed, the width dynamics are $\dot\sigma = -c\,\sigma^3$ with
$c = \tfrac{\eta}{2} w\,|\Delta \Lambda(\xi)|$ in closed form, so
\[
  \sigma(t) = \big(\sigma(0)^{-2} + 2ct\big)^{-1/2} = \Theta(t^{-1/2}),
  \qquad
  \varepsilon(t) = \Theta(1/t).
\]
\end{corollary}

These are sharp enough to test directly, and \texttt{check\_scale\_law.py} does.
On \texttt{TWO\_POINT} the predicted free constant is $c = 1.25$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
run ($2\times10^6$ steps, floor $10^{-12}$) & fitted $c$ & predicted $c$ & ratio\\
\hline
free decay & $1.249835$ & $1.25$ & $0.9999$\\
with magnet, atom init & $0.541092$ & $0.540415$ & $1.0013$\\
with magnet, spread init & $0.541092$ & $0.540415$ & $1.0013$\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent Fitting $\sigma^{-2}$ against $t$ gives $R^2 \ge 1 - 3\times10^{-8}$:
the trajectory is affine in $\sigma^{-2}$, exactly as $\dot\sigma = -c\sigma^3$
requires. Measured log--log slopes are $-0.502$ for $\sigma$ (predicted $-0.5$)
and $-1.003$ for $\varepsilon$ (predicted $-1$).

\begin{remark}[The floor is a hypothesis, not an approximation]\label{rem:floor}
It is tempting to read the width floor as an implementation detail that costs
accuracy. It is the opposite on both counts. It buys \emph{breadth}: condition (C)
is imposed from $s^{\min}$ upward rather than from $0$, so a landscape carrying
structure finer than the floor --- ripple, discretisation noise, a Monte-Carlo
estimate --- can have very many peaks at width $0$ and exactly $M$ at the floor.
Such a game is inside the class with a floor and outside it without. It is also
what lets the payoff be merely \emph{bounded and measurable} rather than $C^2$:
everything the algorithm ever evaluates is blurred at width $\ge s^{\min}$, hence
smooth with bounded derivatives no matter how irregular $u$ is. And it costs no
accuracy, because the floor is not what drives the bias down --- the width
dynamics do, and Corollary~\ref{cor:unfl} recovers exactness as a rate. Its only
genuine cost is that atoms closer together than the floor are unresolvable.

Both benefits attach to the \emph{blur} rather than to the policy, so they are the
part of this section that survives a point-mass reading: a policy of point masses
whose gradients come from perturbations at bandwidth $h > 0$ evaluates only
$h$-blurred objects, and inherits the bounded-measurable payoff and the tolerance
of sub-$h$ structure verbatim. What it does not inherit is the sentence before
last --- with $h$ exogenous nothing drives it down, so exactness stops being a rate
and goes back to being a schedule to tune. That is the trade of this part in one
line.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{3.4 What the Gaussian magnet does}

The magnet on the Gaussian heads is the natural thing to reach for when transport
is slow, and its effect is worth stating here because it is not the one usually
assumed.

\begin{lemma}[The magnet is a pure brake; \GC{Lemma~6}]\label{lem:brake}
With magnet temperature $\tau^{\mathrm g}$ refreshed every $T$ steps, set
\[
  \beta \;=\; \frac{1 - e^{-\eta\tau^{\mathrm g}T}}{\eta\tau^{\mathrm g}T}
  \;\in\; (0,1].
\]
Then \emph{both} Gaussian-head coordinates --- means and widths --- are slowed by
the same factor $\beta$. The flow with magnet is the flow without it, under the
time reparameterisation $t \mapsto \beta t$.
\end{lemma}

Since a time reparameterisation cannot change which initialisations eventually
converge, the consequences are stark: asymptotic basins are unchanged, and what
shrinks is finite-horizon reach. At a fixed step budget you arrive at fewer
targets, and the bias at a fixed step count is worse by $1/\beta$. Combined with
Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp}(ii) --- the magnet makes a trap \emph{deeper} --- the
verdict for a curved landscape is that the Gaussian magnet cannot help transport
and can only cost. The factor was checked over a $16\times$ range in
$\eta\tau^{\mathrm g}T$; configurations sharing that product collapse onto the
same constant, confirming it depends on nothing else:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\hline
$\tau^{\mathrm g}$ & $T$ & $\eta\tau^{\mathrm g}T$ & predicted $\beta$ &
  measured ratio\\
\hline
$0.2$ & $50$ & $0.5$ & $0.78694$ & $1.0035$\\
$0.05$ & $200$ & $0.5$ & $0.78694$ & $1.0006$\\
$0.2$ & $200$ & $2.0$ & $0.43233$ & $1.0009$\\
$0.2$ & $800$ & $8.0$ & $0.12496$ & $0.9976$\\
$0.8$ & $200$ & $8.0$ & $0.12496$ & $0.9979$\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{remark}[Where the magnet \emph{is} necessary]\label{rem:flat}
There is one regime in which it is not optional. If the payoff has no self-term
--- $u(a,b) = \ip{\varphi(a)}{C\psi(b)}$, a pure coupling --- then at equilibrium
the landscape is \emph{constant}: $\nabla\Lambda \equiv 0$, and \eqref{eq:star}
fails at every point. There is no gradient to ascend. The magnet's quadratic term
$-\tfrac{\tau^{\mathrm g}}{2}\|a - \bar a\|^2$ supplies the curvature the game does
not have, and (C) holds with one cell and $\rho(r) \propto r$.

So the right object for condition (C) is not the blurred landscape but the
\emph{magnet-regularised} blurred landscape, and curved and flat games are then
two extremes of one condition rather than two cases. The price in the flat case is
real: the target is $\bar a$, the previous iterate, so the atoms are unidentified
and the conclusion degrades from ``the means converge to the atoms'' to ``the
induced measure converges''. And the dichotomy is per \emph{direction}, not per
game: a two-dimensional action can have a well in one coordinate and matching
pennies in the other, and a scalar $\tau^{\mathrm g}$ then pays the brake in both.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{3.5 What Part 3 leaves open}

The opponent is still frozen, so the peaks being found are still best responses
rather than equilibrium atoms. That is Part 4.

\part*{Part 4. Let the opponent move: a chase that damps}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Part 4}
\setcounter{section}{4}
\setcounter{theorem}{0}

\idea{Now unfreeze the opponent. Your landscape is built from the opponent's
policy, so when they move, your target moves --- and you are no longer climbing a
hill, you are chasing one. The Lyapunov argument of Part 2 gains exactly one
term: how far the target ran while you were closing in. So convergence becomes a
question about a chase, and a chase terminates when the round trip contracts: if
your target moves a little when they move, and theirs moves a little when you
move, the whole thing damps. That is the one genuinely game-theoretic condition in
the theory --- there is no counterpart in single-agent optimisation, because there
is no opponent to chase --- and on the codebase's games it holds with three orders
of magnitude to spare.}

Throughout Part 4 both players' means and widths move; the categorical heads stay
frozen, so that the chase can be seen on its own.

\subsection*{4.1 One extra term}

Player $0$'s landscape is $Q_\nu(a) = \int u(a,b)\,d\nu(b)$, so it is built from
the opponent's whole policy. When the opponent's means move, $Q_\nu$ tilts, and
the attractor $\xi_j$ moves with it. The estimate of Part 2 becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:chase}
  \underbrace{r(t+1)}_{\text{distance to target}}
  \;\le\; r(t) \;-\; \underbrace{\eta\,\sigma^2 w\,\rho\big(r(t)\big)}
                                 _{\text{you close in (Part 2)}}
  \;+\; \underbrace{\big\|\xi_j(t+1) - \xi_j(t)\big\|}_{\text{the target ran}} .
\end{equation}
Everything about Part 2 survives verbatim --- same cells, same Lyapunov function,
same contraction --- and the entire content of Part 4 is bounding the last term.

\begin{remark}[The cell does not move; only the target inside it]\label{rem:fixed}
This is the structural point that makes \eqref{eq:chase} usable, and it is where
the present theory departs from its predecessor. The natural first attempt is to
assume ``each mean starts in the basin of its atom''. That hypothesis does not
survive a game: basins of a \emph{moving} landscape are not forward invariant, so
a component sitting in the right basin at time $t$ can find itself in the wrong
one at $t+1$ because the opponent moved --- through no fault of its own, with no
update applied to it.

Condition (C) repairs this by being quantified over \emph{every} opponent policy
the run can visit, with the \emph{same} cells. A cell is then morally the
intersection of the basins over the whole family: the region belonging to atom $j$
no matter what the opponent does. That is what supplies a single Lyapunov function
valid for all time, and it is the load-bearing difference between a condition that
closes and one that does not.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{4.2 When the chase damps}

\begin{lemma}[How far the target runs; \CV{Lemma~6}, \GC{Proposition~2}]
\label{lem:drift}
Let $g_0$ be the sensitivity of player $0$'s attractor to a displacement of
player $1$'s means, and $g_1$ symmetrically. If the payoff has the finite-rank
form \eqref{eq:finiterank} and the landscapes have curvature $\alpha_i$ at their
branch points, then by the implicit function theorem
\[
  g_0 \;\le\; \frac{\|C\|\,\|\nabla\varphi\|\,\|\nabla\psi\|}{\alpha_0},
\]
and symmetrically. \emph{Curvature resists, coupling drives.}
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Two-player transport]\label{thm:part4}
Assume, for both players, Part 2's hypotheses uniformly over the admissible width
range, and that each player's widths are \emph{eventually constant} --- either
frozen, or driven to a floor in finite time, for which Part 3 supplies a
mechanism. Assume also the \emph{small-gain condition}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sg}
  g_0\,g_1 \;<\; 1 .
\end{equation}
Then both players' means converge to their cells' branch points. If in addition
the branch endpoints at the equilibrium opponent and the terminal width are the
equilibrium atoms --- condition (M) --- then the means converge to the atoms, up to
the branch displacement at that width.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Part 4 is indifferent again]\label{rem:part4family}
The hypothesis has been stated as ``the widths are eventually constant'' rather
than ``Part 3 holds'' deliberately. What the chase argument needs from the widths
is only that they stop contributing a disturbance --- which Part 3 delivers for
Gaussians, by Lemma~\ref{lem:arrive}, and which is true \emph{by construction} for
a point-mass policy at a fixed exploration bandwidth, where there is no width
coordinate to move in the first place.

So Part 4 returns to the indifference of Parts 1 and 2, and the two sources of
target motion it must bound --- the opponent's means and, in Part 5, the
opponent's weights --- are the only ones a point-mass reading has at all. In the
Gaussian reading there are two more, each player's own and its opponent's widths;
those are what \eqref{eq:chase} inherits from Part 3 and what
Remark~\ref{rem:throttle} disposes of. Nothing in Lemma~\ref{lem:drift} or
Proposition~\ref{prop:cap} refers to a width: they are statements about how far an
attractor moves when the landscape tilts, and their constants are curvatures and
coupling norms.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}[Idea]
Apply \eqref{eq:chase} to each player, and note the last term of each is
controlled by the \emph{other} player's tracking error, with constant $g_i$. So
the pair of errors satisfies a two-dimensional comparison system whose off-diagonal
product is $g_0g_1$; \eqref{eq:sg} makes a weighted maximum of the two a Lyapunov
function for the pair. The point that makes this work rather than merely look
plausible is that each player's \emph{step} is proportional to its own tracking
error (Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}), so the disturbance each injects into the other
vanishes as they arrive --- if the steps were merely $O(\eta)$, the two would
hover forever within $O(\eta)$ of their targets instead of converging.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Where the peaks become Nash atoms]\label{rem:M}
Condition (M) is the bridge that Parts 2 and 3 kept deferring, and it is worth
seeing why it is the natural hypothesis rather than a convenient one. By the
support characterisation of a best response, the equilibrium atoms are
\emph{automatically} maxima of the landscape at the equilibrium opponent --- that
is a theorem, not an assumption. So (M) is not asserting anything about where the
atoms are; it is asserting the converse pairing, that the $M$ attractors the class
provides are those maxima and not others. Combined with Part 2's insistence that
there are no \emph{other} maxima, the two conditions between them say: the
landscape's peaks and the equilibrium's atoms are the same finite set.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{4.3 The condition in closed form, with numbers}

\begin{proposition}[Curvature dominates coupling, for the small gain;
\GC{Proposition~2}]\label{prop:cap}
Under the finite-rank form \eqref{eq:finiterank}, the small-gain condition
\eqref{eq:sg} is implied by
\[
  \alpha_0\,\alpha_1 \;>\; \|C\|^2\,\sup\|\nabla\varphi\|\,\sup\|\nabla\psi\| .
\]
\end{proposition}

The same structural assumption \eqref{eq:finiterank} therefore does two jobs, and
they should not be run together. In Part 2 it collapsed the quantifier over
opponent policies and gave Proposition~\ref{prop:capC}, a certificate for
\emph{(C)}, whose constant is $\inf\rho_0$ over the whole cell and which is
correspondingly crude (Remark~\ref{rem:conservative}). Here it bounds the branch
drift and gives a certificate for \emph{(D)}, whose constant is the curvature
$\alpha_i$ \emph{at} the branch. Different conditions, different constants, one
hypothesis on the payoff --- and it is this second one that holds with room to
spare on the codebase's games.

For the codebase's games $u = D(a) - D(b) + c\ip{f(a)}{f(b)}$ with Gaussian bumps
of height $h$ and width $w$, so $\alpha \approx h/w^2$ and the condition reads
$c\,\|f'\|^2 < (h/w^2)^2$ --- an $O(h/w^2)$ cap on the coupling. Concretely, on
\texttt{TWO\_POINT} ($h=1$, $w=0.1$, so $\alpha = 100$; $f(a) = 2a-1$, so
$\|f'\| = 2$; $c = 1$):
\[
  g_0 \;=\; \frac{c\|f'\|}{\alpha} \;=\; \frac{2}{100} \;=\; 0.02,
  \qquad
  g_0 g_1 \;=\; 4\times10^{-4} \;\lll\; 1 ,
\]
and the condition would first bite at $c \approx 50$. The coupling in that game is
therefore three orders of magnitude away from being able to destabilise the
chase --- which is why no experiment at $c=1$ has ever shown coupling-induced
trouble, and why the two players' targets visibly settle rather than chase. A
consistency check on the three-peak game predicts trouble from $c \approx 78$
against a measured degradation between $c = 32$ and $c = 300$.

\begin{remark}[This condition has no single-agent ancestor]\label{rem:gt}
Parts 2 and 3 are, at bottom, graduated optimisation: star-concavity and a
shrinking blur are borrowed from a single-agent literature. Part 4 is not. There
is no opponent in graduated optimisation, hence no moving target, hence no
round-trip gain and no small-gain condition. If one wants to name what the game
setting adds to the optimisation story, it is exactly two things: the uniformity
of (C) over opponent policies (Remark~\ref{rem:fixed}) and the condition
\eqref{eq:sg}.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{4.4 What Part 4 leaves open}

The categorical heads have been frozen. Unfreezing them is not free, because the
opponent's \emph{weights} also enter your landscape: their weight error displaces
your target just as their mean error does. So the chase of Part 4 is really a
four-way chase, and that is Part 5.

\part*{Part 5. All of them at once}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Part 5}
\setcounter{section}{5}
\setcounter{theorem}{0}

\idea{Four things are moving --- two players' means, two players' weights --- and
each disturbs the others. Part 1 says the weights would be fine if the means
stopped; Part 4 says the means would be fine if the weights stopped. Neither
stops. The question is whether the whole interconnection settles, and it is
answered by a small-gain argument: form the matrix of ``how much block $i$ is
disturbed per unit of block $j$'s error, relative to how fast block $i$ fixes
itself'', and require its spectral radius to be below one. Three observations
make that condition checkable, and one of them is what turns the whole scheme
from plausible into provable.}

\subsection*{5.1 The three observations}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Every block's step is proportional to its own error.} A mean moves
  fast only when it is far from its target, because the target is a critical point
  (Lemma~\ref{lem:crit}); the same holds for the categorical head. So the
  disturbance each block injects into its neighbours \emph{vanishes as that block
  arrives}. Without this, the four blocks would settle into a permanent mutual
  jitter of size $O(\eta)$ rather than converging.
\item \textbf{The preconditioner cancels.} The factor $\sigma^2$ slows the mean
  update as the widths collapse --- and slows every disturbance it is trying to
  reject by at least as much (Remark~\ref{rem:throttle}). Around any cycle of the
  interconnection it appears once in a numerator and once in a denominator. So
  the stability condition contains no $\sigma$ and no step size: it is a statement
  about the \emph{game}, checkable before training. (This observation exists to
  answer an objection that only the Gaussian reading raises: it is the width
  collapse of Part 3 that threatens to throttle transport. With a fixed
  preconditioner and no width coordinate there is nothing to cancel and the
  conclusion is immediate --- the same conclusion, reached for free.)
\item \textbf{The categorical temperature is free.} It controls how stiff the
  weight block's target is against the means moving underneath it --- larger
  $\tau$, less sensitive --- and by Lemma~\ref{lem:mmd}(ii) it does not move the
  limit. So it can be turned up until the loop closes, at no asymptotic cost.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{5.2 The composition}

\begin{lemma}[Small gain; \CV{Lemma~10}]\label{lem:sg}
Let four nonnegative errors satisfy
\[
  x_i(t+1) \;\le\; (1 - a_i)\,x_i(t) \;+\; \sum_{j\ne i} b_{ij}\,x_j(t)
  \;+\; d_i(t),
\]
and let $\hat\Gamma_{ij} := b_{ij}/a_i$ --- disturbance received, per unit of the
receiver's own repair rate. If the spectral radius of $\hat\Gamma$ is below one
then there is a weighted maximum $V = \max_i x_i/\pi_i$ that contracts, and
$x(t) \to 0$ whenever the exogenous inputs $d(t)$ do.
\end{lemma}

The four blocks are the two mean errors and the two weight errors; the widths are
not a fifth block, because by Lemma~\ref{lem:arrive} they reach the floor in
\emph{finite time} and thereafter contribute exactly zero, which is what makes
$d(t)$ vanish.

\begin{theorem}[Convergence on the class; \CV{Theorem~1}]\label{thm:main}
Assume Parts 2--4's conditions --- which already include \textbf{(L)}, the linear
star-concavity modulus $\rho(r) \ge \alpha r$ of \eqref{eq:Lpart2}, in force since
Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}(b) --- together with one further condition:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
\item \textbf{(G)} the four-block gain matrix has spectral radius below one.
\end{itemize}
Then every mean converges to its cell's branch point, every width to its floor,
the component matrix to the smoothed atom matrix, and the categorical heads to its
Nash equilibrium; the means are within $O(s^2)$ of the atoms, and the induced
policy pair is $\varepsilon$-Nash with
$\varepsilon = O\big((s^{\min}_0)^2 + (s^{\min}_1)^2\big)$. Released from the
floor, $\varepsilon = \Theta(1/t)$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection*{5.3 The two hard hypotheses, honestly}

\begin{remark}[What (L) costs, and where it was actually spent]\label{rem:L}
(L) is one of the two hypotheses in this development that can exclude a game, and
on a bounded cell it is equivalent to requiring that the branch maxima be
nondegenerate, uniformly. It does \emph{not} require $\alpha$ to be large: for
Gaussian-bump landscapes $\alpha$ is exponentially small at long range, and every
conclusion above is uniform in $\alpha > 0$ --- only the rate degrades
(Remark~\ref{rem:rho}).

It is worth being exact about when it was incurred, because the natural
presentation --- ``Part 5 adds (L) to buy a geometric rate'' --- understates it.
(L) was already required in Part 2, by Theorem~\ref{thm:part2}(b): the
\emph{fixed-step} iteration does not converge without it, for the reason set out
in Remark~\ref{rem:needL}. What Part 5 adds is not the hypothesis but the
observation that one constant pays for three things --- the geometric contraction
of the mean block, the uniform branch curvature $\kappa$ that
Lemma~\ref{lem:arrive} needs for the scales to descend, and the denominators of
the gain matrix in (G). Had (L) been introduced here it would look like a
convenience; introduced in Part 2, it looks like what it is, the price of running
a discrete algorithm rather than a flow.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[What (G) costs]\label{rem:G}
(G) is the quantitative form of Part 4's $g_0g_1 < 1$, extended from two blocks to
four. By observation~2 of \S5.1 it contains no step size and no width, so it is a
$4\times4$ eigenvalue computation from game constants. Its two binding cycles are
the mean$\leftrightarrow$mean cycle --- which is Proposition~\ref{prop:cap}, with
condition numbers made explicit --- and a mean$\leftrightarrow$weight cycle, which
carries a factor $1/\tau$ and is therefore satisfied by taking the categorical
temperature large. Observation~3 is what makes that free.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{5.4 What is still not proved}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Exact gradients.} Everything assumes the payoff and its derivatives
  are available exactly. With sampled estimates the mean block acquires a noise
  term that does \emph{not} vanish with the tracking error, so the argument would
  deliver an $O(\sqrt\eta)$ ball rather than convergence; a decreasing step size
  or variance reduction would be needed. This is the largest remaining gap between
  the theorem and the implementation, and it is the gap that widens fastest if the
  component family is changed --- see \S2.6, item~4.
\item \textbf{Verifying (C) at scale.} Treated in \S2.5, and the summary is that
  the two quantifiers in (C) are collapsed by a finite-rank coupling
  (Proposition~\ref{prop:capC}) or by one-dimensional mode counting
  (Remark~\ref{rem:check}), and not otherwise. Neither route is available for a
  structureless payoff in dimension above one, and the closed-form certificate is
  crude even when it applies (Remark~\ref{rem:conservative}). This is the
  hypothesis with the widest gap between what is assumed and what can be
  confirmed.
\item \textbf{Exploration between cells.} Unchanged from Part 2: covering is
  assumed because the algorithm cannot achieve it.
\item \textbf{Extensive form.} A second theory, not a corollary: the payoff is
  bilinear in sequence form, but the policy parameterises behavioural strategies
  per infoset, and the cellwise analysis would have to be redone with continuation
  values as the landscape.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Reading the five parts together}

The one-sentence version of the whole development is that the problem factorises
and the two factors are not equally hard. Mixing is free: the categorical head
plays a finite matrix game exactly, and finite matrix games are solved
unconditionally (Part 1). Location is everything: it is local ascent on a blurred
landscape, so it succeeds exactly when each region of the action space has one
attractor (Part 2), it self-anneals onto the true peaks as the blur collapses
(Part 3), it survives the opponent moving provided curvature dominates coupling
(Part 4), and the pieces compose provided the round-trip gains are contractions
(Part 5).

The negative result is what fixes the shape. A single spurious attractor produces
an exponentially stable non-Nash configuration with an open basin, immune to the
magnet, to extra capacity and to annealing. So no initialisation-robust class can
permit one, and ``exactly $M$ attractors'' is forced rather than chosen. Which is
the practical message: the failures usually attributed to tuning are properties of
the game, and --- by Remark~\ref{rem:check} --- they are checkable before training.

There is a second thread running through the five parts, and it is worth
collecting because it is invisible from inside any one of them. The Gaussian
enters the development \emph{once}, at Lemma~\ref{lem:heat}, and everything on
either side of that line is indifferent to it. Parts 1 and 2 hold for any family
of components, point masses included, and the exposition says so at the two places
it could be doubted (Remarks~\ref{rem:provenance} and~\ref{rem:trapfamily}); Parts
4 and 5 hold for anything that eventually stops moving its widths
(Remark~\ref{rem:part4family}). What Part 3 buys with the choice is the heat
equation, and what the heat equation buys is a width that anneals itself; what it
costs is the $O(s^2)$ representation bias that a point-mass family would not have
had (Remark~\ref{rem:biasfamily}). That the mechanism drives its own cost to zero
at $\Theta(1/t)$ is the content of Corollary~\ref{cor:unfl}, and it is the reason
the trade is worth making rather than merely defensible.

Two riders on that, both stated where they arise and repeated here because they
are the places a careful reader will push. The cheap certificate for condition (C)
--- the mode count of Remark~\ref{rem:check} --- is Gaussian and one-dimensional
even though the theorem it certifies is neither; and the exact-gradient assumption
of \S5.4 is an idealisation of something real under the Gaussian reading and of
nothing in particular under a point-mass one. Neither changes a theorem. Both
change how far a theorem is from an implementation, which is the more useful thing
to know.

\end{document}
